 85	\begin{lemma}[The low-degree question distribution is a typed CL distribution]\label{lem:ld-question-typed-cl}
 86		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.ldQuestionDistribution_eq_typedCL}
 87		% No completion mark: the linked declaration proves the distribution identity only, not the typed-family assertion. See the last paragraph of the statement; the open family assertion is tracked by issue 180.
 88		\uses{def:ld-question-distribution, def:typed-cl-distributions, def:graph-distribution, lem:ld-aline-level, lem:ld-dline-level}
 89		Fix $\mathsf{ldparams} = (q,m,d,k)$ as in Definition~\ref{def:ld-game}, let $L = \{L_t\}_{t \in \mathcal T^{\mathrm{ld}}}$ be the family of Definition~\ref{def:ld-question-distribution}, and let $G^{\mathrm{ld}}$ be the graph on $\mathcal T^{\mathrm{ld}}$ all of whose unordered pairs, self-loops included, are edges. Then the question distribution of $\mathfrak{G}^{\mathrm{ld}}$ is the $(\mathcal T^{\mathrm{ld}}, G^{\mathrm{ld}})$-typed conditionally linear distribution $\mu^{G^{\mathrm{ld}}}_{L,\,L}$ of Definition~\ref{def:typed-cl-distributions}. The linked declaration proves the distribution identity: the question distribution of $\mathfrak{G}^{\mathrm{ld}}$ equals the distribution that the construction of Definition~\ref{def:typed-cl-distributions} produces from the pair $(L, L)$ and the graph $G^{\mathrm{ld}}$, a construction that is available for an arbitrary family of maps. It does not prove that $L$ is a $\mathcal T^{\mathrm{ld}}$-typed family of conditionally linear functions of one common level, which is what makes the right-hand side a typed conditionally linear distribution in the sense of Definition~\ref{def:typed-cl-distributions}: Lemmas~\ref{lem:ld-aline-level} and~\ref{lem:ld-dline-level} give the levels of $L_{\mathsf{ALine}}$ and $L_{\mathsf{DLine}}$, while the level of $L_{\mathsf{Point}}$ and the passage of the three maps to a common level are not formalized. That family assertion is open and is tracked as issue~\#180, so the statement and the proof below carry no completion mark.
 90	\end{lemma}
 91	\begin{proof}
 92		% No completion mark: the Lean proof establishes the distribution identity only; the typed-family hypotheses on L are open and tracked by issue 180.
 93		\uses{def:typed-cl-distributions, def:graph-distribution, def:cl-dist, lem:uniform-bind-map, lem:distribution-map-comp}
 94		Every unordered pair of types is an edge of $G^{\mathrm{ld}}$, so the graph distribution $\mu_{G^{\mathrm{ld}}}$ of Definition~\ref{def:graph-distribution} is the uniform distribution on $\mathcal T^{\mathrm{ld}} \times \mathcal T^{\mathrm{ld}}$. For each ordered type pair $(t_{\mathrm A}, t_{\mathrm B})$ the CL distribution $\mu_{L_{t_{\mathrm A}},\, L_{t_{\mathrm B}}}$ is the push-forward of the uniform distribution on $V$ along $z \mapsto \bigl(L_{t_{\mathrm A}}(z), L_{t_{\mathrm B}}(z)\bigr)$. Binding the uniform type pair to these push-forwards and tagging each content with its type is exactly the two-step sampling procedure of Definition~\ref{def:ld-question-distribution}. This establishes the distribution identity; the typed-family hypotheses on $L$ are not established here.
